  "result": "I implemented `slugify` in `src/strings.js` and added 11 tests to `test/strings.test.js`. `npm test` passes all 13 tests: the 2 that were already there plus the new ones.\n\nHow it works:\n1. It lowercases the text, splits accented letters into the plain letter plus the accent mark, and then deletes the accent marks. That handles Vietnamese and most other Latin text.\n2. A few letters don't split that way, so it maps them by hand. `đ`/`Đ` becomes `d`, as you asked.\n3. Each run of characters that aren't `a-z` or `0-9` becomes one hyphen, and hyphens at the start and end are removed.\n4. It cuts the slug to 50 characters and then removes any hyphens left at the end.\n\nTwo things you might not expect:\n- **Extra letter mappings:** I also map `ð`→d, `ø`→o, `ł`→l, `ß`→ss, `æ`→ae, `œ`→oe and `þ`→th, beyond the `đ` rule you gave. Without this they would turn into hyphens, so `Øresund` would become `resund` instead of `oresund`. The list is easy to remove if you'd rather stick to the stated rules.\n- **Short slugs at the cut:** if the 50th character is a hyphen, the slug ends up 49 characters long, not 50.\n\nThe new tests cover Vietnamese text, `đ`/`Đ`, other accented Latin text, collapsing runs of symbols, trimming hyphens, digits, empty input, and the 50-character limit. That includes a cut that lands on a hyphen and a slug of exactly 50 characters.",
